window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-dccd5924f08b9ad8"]={"zh":"<h1 id=\"agent-security\">Agent Security（智能体安全）</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>在管理控制台（Admin Console）中使用 Agent Security 管理策略和配置。它取代了“策略与配置”（Policies &amp; Configuration）。Agent Security 的推出与 Work 和 dots 的本地电脑访问功能相互独立。</p> <p>符合条件的旧版云策略迁移时，其设置会转入 Global，同时保留策略分配和优先级顺序。请在 Agent Security 中检查迁移后的策略。Work 和 dots 的本地电脑访问需要另行选择启用。</p> <p><a id=\"where-settings-apply\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">设置的适用范围</h2> <p>每项策略都以 Global 基线为起点。环境覆盖设置可修改 Local 或 Codex Cloud 中受支持的执行设置。如果某个环境没有覆盖项，就会继承该策略中适用的 Global 设置。</p> <ul> <li> <p>Global：设置协调器（orchestrator）控制项，包括审批、网页搜索和共享执行设置。</p> </li> <li> <p>Local：调整在已连接电脑上运行的工作所支持的执行设置。</p> </li> <li> <p>Codex Cloud：调整 Codex 云端任务所支持的执行设置。可以在启用 Codex Cloud 之前配置这些策略，但只有在权限页面启用 Codex Cloud 后才会生效。Work Cloud 使用单独的功能权限，详见<a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\">Agent Security 对 Work Cloud 的适用方式</a>。</p> </li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>插图：新的“Work Cloud 本地电脑访问”设置，使 Work 能在云端协调并使用已连接的电脑。Local 的本地协调方式与 Work Cloud 本身保持不变。启用托管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略会通过共享云端协调器生效；设备限制仍然适用，且 MDM 的优先级高于 Agent Security。Work 云端容器使用独立于本地执行策略的执行配置和要求。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"set-a-baseline-and-add-environment-settings\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">设置基线并添加环境配置</h2> <ol> <li> <p>打开 Agent Security，检查现有 Global 策略、分配对象和优先级顺序，并确认它们符合组织预期的控制要求。此项检查不会启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问。</p> </li> <li> <p>分别检查“要求”（Requirements）和“默认值”（Defaults）。要求用于设定用户无法覆盖的限制；默认值则是在这些限制范围内设定初始值。</p> </li> <li> <p>将协调器控制项保留在 Global 中，包括审批要求、允许使用的网页搜索模式和托管工具控制项。</p> </li> <li> <p>在 Local 或 Codex Cloud 环境中添加受支持的执行控制设置，例如沙盒、文件系统权限和托管执行网络。如需限制到特定操作系统，请使用操作系统专属的覆盖设置。</p> </li> <li> <p>保存策略，并检查系统针对所填设置显示的验证信息。</p> </li> </ol> <p><a id=\"choose-controls-and-configuration-fields\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">选择控制项和配置字段</h2> <p>协调器负责统筹任务。执行器（executor）是运行某个执行步骤的电脑或云端容器。请在 Global 中配置协调器控制项。环境级 <code>requirements.toml</code> 设置仅支持执行控制。审批策略、网页搜索等协调器设置必须留在 Global，不能由环境覆盖。</p> <h3 id=\"orchestrator-controls\">协调器控制项（Orchestrator controls）</h3> <p>请在 Global 中配置以下控制项。对于启用了本地访问的 Work 和 dots，启用托管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略会通过共享云端协调器生效。环境覆盖仅适用于受支持的执行设置，不能覆盖协调器控制项。下表所列的审批、网页搜索、应用、MCP、插件和规则要求中，只有 <strong>允许的审批策略（Allowed approval policies）</strong> 和 <strong>允许的网页搜索模式（Allowed web search modes）</strong> 在 Agent Security 界面中有专用控制项。其他字段请通过 TOML 配置。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>控制项</th> <th>控制内容</th> <th><code>requirements.toml</code> 字段</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>审批策略与审核</td> <td>智能体何时需要审批、由谁审核，包括自动审核。</td> <td><code>allowed_approval_policies</code><br><code>allowed_approvals_reviewers</code><br><code>auto_review</code><br><code>guardian_policy_config</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>网页搜索模式</td> <td>智能体可以使用哪些网页搜索模式。</td> <td><code>allowed_web_search_modes</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>应用、MCP 服务器和 <code>plugins</code></td> <td>可用的应用、MCP 服务器和插件，以及相应配置。</td> <td><code>apps</code><br><code>mcp_servers</code><br><code>plugins</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>命令 <code>rules</code></td> <td>智能体可以运行哪些命令、哪些命令需要审批，以及哪些命令禁止运行。</td> <td><code>rules</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>托管 <code>hooks</code></td> <td>管理员在受支持的任务和工具事件中定义的操作。</td> <td><code>hooks</code><br><code>allow_managed_hooks_only</code></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"hooks-in-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"work-cloud\">Work Cloud 本地电脑访问中的钩子</h3> <p>启用托管策略和远程钩子后，启用了本地访问的 Work Cloud 和 dots 会在云端协调器上使用管理员托管的远程 MCP 钩子。请在 Global <code>requirements.toml</code> 中配置 <code>mcp_tool</code> 处理程序。未启用本地访问的 Work Cloud 和个人账户不会使用这些企业钩子。云端协调不支持命令/终端、提示词和智能体处理程序；也不支持来自本地配置、插件或本地目录的钩子、限定到某个环境的钩子，以及 <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP 钩子，即使工具是在本地执行也不支持。当协调和执行都在本地进行时，现有的受支持钩子仍可用于仅限本地的 Work 和 Codex 线程。管理员仍可在 Agent Security 中为这些工作流配置受支持的托管钩子。</p> <p>依赖这些钩子之前，请测试回调连接、所需事件和失败时的行为。明确且受支持的拒绝结果可以阻止操作；但 <code>PreToolUse</code> 回调出错、超时或返回格式错误时，钩子可能失败而不阻止工具运行。MCP 钩子不能提供完整的 Compliance API 审计记录。</p> <p>Global 中还包含桌面端和客户端设置，其中一些设置仅适用于桌面应用。在 Global 中配置某个设置，并不意味着它会在所有环境中生效。各字段的适用范围请参阅 Configuration Reference。</p> <p><a id=\"execution-controls-executor\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_4\">执行控制（执行器）</h3> <p>以下字段控制工作如何在电脑或云端容器中运行。请在 Global 中设置共享值；如需在 Local 或 Codex Cloud 中采用不同设置，再为该环境添加受支持的覆盖值。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>控制项</th> <th>控制内容</th> <th><code>requirements.toml</code> 字段</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>登录 Shell</td> <td>Shell 工具能否启动登录 Shell。</td> <td><code>allow_login_shell</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>允许的沙盒模式</td> <td>执行器可以使用哪些沙盒模式。</td> <td><code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>权限配置文件与默认值</td> <td>允许使用的权限配置文件、访问限制以及默认配置文件。</td> <td><code>allowed_permission_profiles</code><br><code>default_permissions</code><br><code>permissions</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>远程沙盒配置</td> <td>根据主机名选择的主机专属沙盒模式。</td> <td><code>remote_sandbox_config</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>托管执行网络</td> <td>托管网络访问，包括允许和拒绝的目标地址。</td> <td><code>experimental_network</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Windows 执行设置</td> <td>Windows 上特定于平台的执行和沙盒设置。</td> <td><code>windows</code></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>此表列出了支持环境覆盖的字段组。每组中受支持的选项可能因平台而异；有些要求会跨策略合并，而非彼此取代。支持的取值请参阅 Configuration Reference；网络例外情况请参阅 Managed configuration。</p> <p>在受支持的托管 Codex Cloud 执行路径中，Agent Security 要求会限制命令行网络访问。Codex Cloud 环境的互联网设置另行生效。即使某个域名在 Agent Security 中获准访问，也不能覆盖 Cloud 环境互联网设置中的限制。这些命令行网络控制本身不会关闭托管网页搜索、应用或 MCP。ChatGPT Work Cloud 使用单独的功能权限，不会继承这些 Agent Security 要求。</p> <p>托管命令允许列表适用于通过托管代理运行的命令。如果策略允许完全提升沙盒权限且该操作已获批准，执行过程可能绕过命令代理。有限的网络访问授权不等同于完全提升沙盒权限。请根据预期边界配置强制审批与沙盒要求，并分别测试普通命令和提升权限后的命令。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-networking-in-the-ui\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">在界面中配置网络</h3> <ol> <li>打开 <strong>管理控制台（Admin Console）&gt; Agent Security</strong>。选择一项策略，再选择 <strong>Global</strong>、<strong>Local</strong> 或 <strong>Codex Cloud</strong>。共享基线使用 Global；受支持的差异设置则使用环境覆盖。</li> <li>打开 <strong>要求（Requirements）</strong> 并启用 <strong>管理网络（Manage Networking）</strong>。添加所需域名，并为每个域名选择 <strong>允许（Allow）</strong> 或 <strong>拒绝（Deny）</strong>。如果不希望普通用户配置或按域名审批扩大托管代理的允许列表，请启用 <strong>仅允许管理员添加的域名（Only allow domains added by admins）</strong>。</li> <li>保存前检查最终生效的设置和继承规则。环境设置为空时会继承 Global，而不是清除 Global 设置。Codex Cloud 的本地/私有网络连接能力需要单独检查。关闭“管理网络”并不等于关闭 Cloud 环境的互联网访问。</li> <li>对 Codex Cloud，还要检查环境的互联网访问、目标地址和访问方式设置。保存后，分别测试预期允许的请求和预期阻止的请求。如策略允许完全提升沙盒权限，也要单独测试该情形。</li> </ol> <p><a id=\"no-effective-allowed-destinations\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_6\">没有任何实际允许的目标地址</h4> <p>启用 <strong>管理网络（Manage Networking）</strong> 和 <strong>仅允许管理员添加的域名（Only allow domains added by admins）</strong> 后，普通托管命令必须存在实际生效的允许目标地址。如果没有配置或继承任何 Allow 条目，这些命令就没有可访问的目标地址。只有拒绝规则的策略不会自动允许访问互联网的其他部分。请添加必要的 Allow 条目，并在保存前检查继承规则。此限制适用于托管命令代理，不适用于所有工具或获批的完整沙盒权限提升。</p> <p><a id=\"codex-cloud-localprivate-connectivity\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"codex-cloud\">Codex Cloud 的本地/私有网络连接</h4> <p>如果本地/私有网络连接明确设置为 Off，即使目标域名已获准，Codex Cloud 也可能无法连接其上游代理。请检查最终的 <code>allow_local_binding</code> 值，并确认是哪项策略或设置提供了该值。在受支持的 Cloud 代理路径中，只有当适用要求、所选网络配置文件和代理功能设置均未提供该值时，默认值才是 true。继承而来的 false 仍然算作明确设置。在支持的情况下，可设置优先级更高的 Cloud 覆盖值，仅更改 Codex Cloud 的此项设置，而不影响 Local 使用的 Global 值。这不会添加任何域名 Allow 条目。依赖此覆盖设置前，请先确认执行器支持。不要将 Cloud 的此默认行为套用到 Local。</p> <p><a id=\"environment-defaults\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">环境默认值</h3> <p>“默认值”（Defaults）编辑器使用 <code>config.toml</code> 字段，与 <code>requirements.toml</code> 约束不同。受支持的顶层环境默认值包括：</p> <ul> <li> <p>Shell 行为：<code>allow_login_shell</code> 和 <code>shell_environment_policy</code>。</p> </li> <li> <p>沙盒和权限：<code>sandbox_mode</code>、<code>sandbox_workspace_write</code>、<code>default_permissions</code> 和权限。</p> </li> <li> <p>Windows 执行：windows。</p> </li> </ul> <p>默认值不会覆盖强制要求。审批和网页搜索等协调器默认设置应保留在 Global 中。</p> <p><a id=\"understand-how-policies-combine\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">了解策略如何组合</h2> <ul> <li> <p>不同策略之间，高优先级策略会覆盖低优先级策略，即使低优先级策略更加具体也一样。</p> </li> <li> <p>在同一项策略内，受支持的执行设置按以下顺序确定：操作系统专属环境覆盖、适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖，最后是 Global。</p> </li> <li> <p>对于本地执行，MDM 和旧版受管设备要求的优先级高于 Agent Security；设备的系统要求文件优先级则低于 Agent Security。</p> </li> </ul> <p>在同一项策略中，管理员设置的环境覆盖可以允许 Global 中拒绝的域名，也可以拒绝 Global 中允许的域名。没有环境覆盖时，就继承 Global 规则。其他实际生效的 Deny 规则或访问控制仍可能阻止请求。</p> <p>上述结论仅适用于同一策略中相同的域名键，且不存在改变结果的更高优先级策略。更高优先级的值会替换相同键的值，其他继承的键仍然保留。Allow 不会绕过另一条匹配的 Deny 规则（例如继承的通配符规则）。环境映射为空，也不会清除继承规则。</p> <p><a id=\"how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"agent-security-work-cloud\">Agent Security 对 Work Cloud 的适用方式</h2> <p>在 <strong>管理控制台（Admin Console）&gt; 权限与角色（Permissions &amp; roles）&gt; 工作区功能（Workspace capabilities）&gt; 云端电脑功能（Cloud computer capabilities）</strong> 下配置 <strong>云端浏览器使用（Cloud browser use）</strong> 和 <strong>云端网络访问（Cloud network access）</strong>。Work Cloud 和 dots 共用这些功能权限；它们可以独立于 Work Cloud 访问权限进行配置。Work 任务仍须拥有 Work 访问权限，以及使用所需每项功能的权限。浏览器访问和代码/终端网络访问应分别检查；关闭其中一项不会自动关闭另一项。</p> <p>Work Cloud 容器和 dots 云端电脑使用各自的执行配置和要求，不使用为其他类型执行器准备的托管环境配置包。本地文件和网络限制不会自动应用到这些云端电脑。受支持的 Global 协调器策略具有单独的作用范围。Global 策略或 Codex Cloud 覆盖设置不会配置共享云端功能权限；请另行检查这些权限。</p> <p>核验成员的实际访问权限时，请检查工作区默认设置、直接分配和通过群组分配的所有角色，以及 Lockdown Mode 等单独强制执行的限制。</p> <p>启用 Work Cloud 下的 <strong>允许本地电脑访问（Allow local computer access）</strong> 之前，请检查 Global 基线，并确认与你组织依赖的各项控制兼容。<strong>在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中本地使用 Codex（Use Codex locally on the ChatGPT desktop app）</strong> 并非前置条件。如果任何云策略启用了 <code>enforce_residency</code>，Work 和 dots 的 <strong>允许本地电脑访问</strong> 都会被禁用。此安全措施不会配置工作区数据驻留，也不会单独关闭 Work Cloud 或 dots。单独的设置步骤、资格和连接行为请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad\">Work Cloud 和 dots 的本地电脑访问</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"policy-api-and-terraform\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"policy-api-terraform\">Policy API 与 Terraform</h2> <p>使用 Policy API 管理 Global 设置；管理 Local 或 Codex Cloud 设置时，请使用 Agent Security 界面。迁移后，现有 Global API 工作流仍可使用。请测试脚本和 Terraform 集成，并确认策略分配和优先级顺序没有改变。</p> <p>策略迁移不会更改 SCIM 成员同步或 RBAC 角色。</p> <p><a id=\"related-guides\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">相关指南</h2> <ul> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>：策略下发、优先级以及字段专属的合并行为。</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>：字段定义及与 Work 的兼容性。</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad\">Work Cloud 和 dots 的本地电脑访问</a>：前置条件和设置步骤。</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">角色和工作区权限</a>：Work 和 Codex 功能访问权限。</p> </li> </ul>","en":"<h1 id=\"agent-security\">Agent Security</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Use Agent Security in the Admin Console to manage policies and configuration. It replaces Policies &amp; Configuration. Its rollout is independent of local computer access for Work and dots.</p> <p>When eligible legacy cloud policies migrate, their settings carry over into Global, preserving policy assignments and ordering. Review the migrated policies in Agent Security. Local computer access is a separate opt-in for Work and for dots.</p> <h2 id=\"where-settings-apply\">Where settings apply</h2> <p>Each policy starts with a Global baseline. Environment overrides change supported execution settings for Local or Codex Cloud. Where an environment has no override, it inherits the applicable Global settings from that policy.</p> <ul> <li> <p>Global: set orchestrator controls, including approvals and web search, and shared execution settings.</p> </li> <li> <p>Local: adjust supported execution settings for work that runs on a connected computer.</p> </li> <li> <p>Codex Cloud: adjust supported execution settings for Codex cloud tasks. You can configure these policies before enabling Codex Cloud, but they apply only after you enable Codex Cloud on the permissions page. Work Cloud has separate capability permissions, described in <a href=\"#/article/a-dccd5924f08b9ad8?anchor=how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\">How Agent Security applies to Work Cloud</a>.</p> </li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: The new Local computer access with Work Cloud setting adds cloud-orchestrated Work on a connected computer. Local orchestration and Work Cloud remain unchanged. Supported Global policy applies through the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled; device restrictions still apply, with MDM above Agent Security. Work cloud containers use their own execution configuration and requirements, separate from local execution policy.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"set-a-baseline-and-add-environment-settings\">Set a baseline and add environment settings</h2> <ol> <li> <p>Open Agent Security and review your existing Global policies, assignments, and ordering. Check them against your organization's intended controls. This review does not enable Local computer access with Work Cloud.</p> </li> <li> <p>Review Requirements and Defaults separately. Requirements set limits that users cannot override. Defaults set starting values within those limits.</p> </li> <li> <p>Keep orchestrator controls in Global. These include approval requirements, allowed web search modes, and managed tool controls.</p> </li> <li> <p>Add Local or Codex Cloud environment settings for supported execution controls, such as sandboxing, filesystem permissions, and managed execution networking. Use an operating-system-specific override when that scope is needed.</p> </li> <li> <p>Save the policy and review any validation messages about the settings you entered.</p> </li> </ol> <h2 id=\"choose-controls-and-configuration-fields\">Choose controls and configuration fields</h2> <p>The orchestrator coordinates the task. An executor is the computer or cloud container that runs an execution step. Configure orchestrator controls in Global. Environment <code>requirements.toml</code> settings support execution controls only. Orchestrator settings, such as approval policy and web search, stay in Global and cannot be overridden by an environment.</p> <h3 id=\"orchestrator-controls\">Orchestrator controls</h3> <p>Configure these controls in Global. For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy applies through the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled. Environment overrides apply to supported execution settings and cannot override orchestrator controls. Of the approval, web search, app, MCP, plugin, and rule requirements listed below, only <strong>Allowed approval policies</strong> and <strong>Allowed web search modes</strong> have dedicated controls in the Agent Security UI. Configure the other fields through TOML.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Control</th> <th>What this controls</th> <th><code>requirements.toml</code> fields</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Approval policies and review</td> <td>When the agent needs approval and who reviews it, including automatic review.</td> <td><code>allowed_approval_policies</code><br><code>allowed_approvals_reviewers</code><br><code>auto_review</code><br><code>guardian_policy_config</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Web search modes</td> <td>Which web search modes the agent may use.</td> <td><code>allowed_web_search_modes</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Apps, MCP servers, and <code>plugins</code></td> <td>Available apps, MCP servers, and plugins, and their configuration.</td> <td><code>apps</code><br><code>mcp_servers</code><br><code>plugins</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Command <code>rules</code></td> <td>Which commands the agent can run, which require approval, and which it cannot run.</td> <td><code>rules</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Managed <code>hooks</code></td> <td>Admin-defined actions at supported task and tool events.</td> <td><code>hooks</code><br><code>allow_managed_hooks_only</code></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h3 id=\"hooks-in-local-computer-access-with-work-cloud\">Hooks in Local computer access with Work Cloud</h3> <p>When managed policy and remote hooks are enabled, Work Cloud with local access and dots use admin-managed remote MCP hooks on the cloud orchestrator. Configure <code>mcp_tool</code> handlers in Global <code>requirements.toml</code>. Work Cloud without local access and personal accounts do not use these enterprise hooks. Command/shell, prompt, and agent handlers; hooks from local configuration, plugins, or local directories; environment-scoped hooks; and <code>SessionEnd</code> MCP hooks are not supported with cloud orchestration, even when tools execute locally. When both orchestration and execution are local, existing supported hooks continue to work in local-only Work and Codex threads. Admins can still configure supported managed hooks in Agent Security for those workflows.</p> <p>Before relying on these hooks, test callback connectivity, required events, and failure behavior. An explicit supported denial can block an action, but a <code>PreToolUse</code> callback error, timeout, or malformed response can fail the hook without blocking the tool. MCP hooks do not provide a complete Compliance API audit trail.</p> <p>Global also contains desktop and client settings. Some of these settings apply only to the desktop app. Configuring a setting in Global does not mean it applies everywhere. See the Configuration Reference for each field's supported use.</p> <h3 id=\"execution-controls-executor\">Execution controls (executor)</h3> <p>These fields control how work runs on a computer or in a cloud container. Set shared values in Global. Use Local or Codex Cloud overrides for supported settings that need to differ in that environment.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Control</th> <th>What this controls</th> <th><code>requirements.toml</code> fields</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Login shell use</td> <td>Whether shell tools can start a login shell.</td> <td><code>allow_login_shell</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Allowed sandbox modes</td> <td>Which sandbox modes the executor can use.</td> <td><code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Permission profiles and defaults</td> <td>Allowed permission profiles, their access limits, and the default profile.</td> <td><code>allowed_permission_profiles</code><br><code>default_permissions</code><br><code>permissions</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Remote sandbox configuration</td> <td>Host-specific sandbox modes, selected by hostname.</td> <td><code>remote_sandbox_config</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Managed execution networking</td> <td>Managed network access, including allowed and denied destinations.</td> <td><code>experimental_network</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Windows execution settings</td> <td>Platform-specific execution and sandbox settings on Windows.</td> <td><code>windows</code></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The table shows which field groups support environment overrides. Supported options within each group can vary by platform, and some requirements combine across policies instead of replacing one another. See the Configuration Reference for supported values and Managed configuration for network exceptions.</p> <p>On supported managed Codex Cloud execution paths, Agent Security requirements constrain command networking. Codex Cloud environment internet settings apply separately. An allowed domain in Agent Security does not override a restriction in the Cloud environment's internet settings. These command-network controls do not, by themselves, disable hosted web search, apps, or MCP. ChatGPT Work Cloud has separate capability permissions and does not inherit these Agent Security requirements.</p> <p>A managed command allowlist applies to commands using the managed proxy. Where policy permits full sandbox escalation and it is approved, that execution can bypass the command proxy. A narrow network grant is different from full sandbox escalation. Configure enforced approval and sandbox requirements for the intended boundary, and test both ordinary and escalated commands.</p> <h3 id=\"configure-networking-in-the-ui\">Configure networking in the UI</h3> <ol> <li>Open <strong>Admin Console &gt; Agent Security</strong>. Select a policy and choose <strong>Global</strong>, <strong>Local</strong>, or <strong>Codex Cloud</strong>. Use Global for the shared baseline and an environment override for supported differences.</li> <li>Open <strong>Requirements</strong> and turn on <strong>Manage Networking</strong>. Add the required domain entries and choose <strong>Allow</strong> or <strong>Deny</strong> for each. Turn on <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> if ordinary user configuration and per-domain approvals must not expand the managed proxy allowlist.</li> <li>Review the effective settings and inherited rules before saving. Empty environment settings inherit Global rather than clearing it. Check local/private connectivity separately for Codex Cloud. Manage Networking Off is not the Cloud environment Internet access Off switch.</li> <li>For Codex Cloud, also check the environment's internet access, destination, and method settings. Save and test an intended allowed request and an intended blocked request. Test any permitted full sandbox escalation separately.</li> </ol> <h4 id=\"no-effective-allowed-destinations\">No effective allowed destinations</h4> <p>When <strong>Manage Networking</strong> and <strong>Only allow domains added by admins</strong> are On, ordinary managed commands need effective allowed destinations. If no Allow entries are configured or inherited, those commands have no allowed destinations. A deny-only policy does not implicitly allow the rest of the internet. Add required Allow entries and check inherited rules before saving. This restriction applies to the managed command proxy, not every tool or approved full sandbox escalation.</p> <h4 id=\"codex-cloud-localprivate-connectivity\">Codex Cloud local/private connectivity</h4> <p>An explicit Off value for local/private connectivity can prevent Codex Cloud from reaching its upstream proxy, even when the destination domain is allowed. Check the final <code>allow_local_binding</code> value and identify which policy or setting supplies it. On the supported Cloud proxy path, this defaults to true only when no applicable requirement, selected network profile, or proxy feature setting supplies a value. An inherited false still counts as an explicit setting. Where supported, set a higher-priority Cloud override to change this value for Codex Cloud without changing the Global value used by Local. This does not add domain Allow entries. Verify executor support before relying on the override. Do not apply this Cloud default to Local.</p> <h3 id=\"environment-defaults\">Environment defaults</h3> <p>The Defaults editor uses <code>config.toml</code> fields, which are different from <code>requirements.toml</code> constraints. Supported top-level environment defaults are:</p> <ul> <li> <p>Shell behavior: <code>allow_login_shell</code> and <code>shell_environment_policy</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>Sandbox and permissions: <code>sandbox_mode</code>, <code>sandbox_workspace_write</code>, <code>default_permissions</code>, and permissions.</p> </li> <li> <p>Windows execution: windows.</p> </li> </ul> <p>A default does not override an enforced requirement. Keep orchestrator defaults, including approval and web search settings, in Global.</p> <h2 id=\"understand-how-policies-combine\">Understand how policies combine</h2> <ul> <li> <p>Across policies, a higher-priority policy wins over a lower-priority policy, even when the lower-priority policy is more specific.</p> </li> <li> <p>Within one policy, supported execution settings resolve in this order: an operating-system-specific environment override, an all-OS environment override, then Global.</p> </li> <li> <p>For local execution, MDM and legacy managed-device requirements rank above Agent Security. The device's system requirements file ranks below Agent Security.</p> </li> </ul> <p>For the same domain rule within a policy, an admin environment override can allow a domain denied in Global or deny a domain allowed in Global. Without an environment override, the Global rule is inherited. Other effective Deny rules or access controls can still block a request.</p> <p>These outcomes compare the same domain key within one policy, with no higher-priority policy changing the result. A higher-priority value replaces the same key. Other inherited keys remain. An Allow does not bypass a different matching Deny, such as an inherited wildcard. An empty environment map does not clear inherited rules.</p> <h2 id=\"how-agent-security-applies-to-work-cloud\">How Agent Security applies to Work Cloud</h2> <p>Configure <strong>Cloud browser use</strong> and <strong>Cloud network access</strong> under <strong>Admin Console &gt; Permissions &amp; roles &gt; Workspace capabilities &gt; Cloud computer capabilities</strong>. These shared capabilities are available to Work Cloud and dots and can be configured independently of Work Cloud access. A Work task still needs Work access and permission to use each capability it requires. Review browser access and code or shell network access separately. Disabling one does not automatically disable the other.</p> <p>Work Cloud containers and dots cloud computers use their own execution configuration and requirements, rather than the managed environment bundle used by other executor types. Local file and network restrictions do not automatically apply to these cloud computers. Supported Global orchestrator policy has a separate scope. A Global policy or Codex Cloud override does not configure shared cloud capability permissions. Review those permissions separately.</p> <p>Check the workspace default, all direct and group-assigned roles, and separately enforced restrictions such as Lockdown Mode when verifying a member's effective access.</p> <p>Before enabling <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> under Work Cloud, review the Global baseline and check compatibility with the controls your organization relies on. <strong>Use Codex locally on the ChatGPT desktop app</strong> is not a prerequisite. If <code>enforce_residency</code> is enabled in any cloud policy, <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> is disabled for both Work and dots. This safeguard does not configure workspace residency or, by itself, disable Work Cloud or dots. Review <a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad\">Local computer access for Work Cloud and dots</a> for separate setup steps, eligibility, and connection behavior.</p> <h2 id=\"policy-api-and-terraform\">Policy API and Terraform</h2> <p>Use the policy API to manage Global settings. To manage Local or Codex Cloud settings, use the Agent Security UI. Existing Global API workflows remain available after migration. Test your scripts and Terraform integrations, and confirm that policy assignments and ordering are unchanged.</p> <p>Policy migration does not change SCIM membership synchronization or RBAC roles.</p> <h2 id=\"related-guides\">Related guides</h2> <ul> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a>: policy delivery, precedence, and field-specific merge behavior.</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a>: field definitions and Work compatibility.</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad\">Local computer access for Work Cloud and dots</a>: prerequisites and setup steps.</p> </li> <li> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-a292ca32ae057ed8\">Roles and workspace permissions</a>: access to Work and Codex capabilities.</p> </li> </ul>"};
